1. Unsupervised extraction of a seed dictionary by exploiting topological similarities between pre-trained monolingual embedding spaces (C1);
2. A self-learning procedure that iteratively refines the dictionary (C2);
3. Preprocessing and postprocessing steps, including unit length normalization, mean centering, and (de)whitening (C3).

An item is considered hate speech if it: 
(1) targets individuals or groups based on their characteristics; 
(2) demonstrates a clear intention to incite harm or promote hatred; 
(3) may or may not use offensive or profane words.

The five considered aspects in hate speech analysis are: 
(a) whether the text is direct or indirect; 
(b) the type of offense (offensive, disrespectful, hateful, fearful out of ignorance, abusive, or normal); 
(c) the attribute based on which it discriminates; 
(d) the name of the targeted group; 
and (e) the annotators' sentiment towards the content.

